WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:05.020
Deadpool 2 is finally here, and with it comes
more Easter eggs than an industrial hatchery.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.980
It's no surprise that Wade Wilson would keep
the pop culture references coming fast and

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.620
furious, but Deadpool 2 also came with a pretty
wide assortment of secrets that you'd have

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.060
to dig pretty far into your comic archives
to find.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.680
Gird your loins and prepare yourself for spoilers:
like a stolen prison pen, we're going deep.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:22.880
X-Stuff

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.880
Deadpool's connection to the X-Men movie universe
isn't a secret… it's just a thing that the

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.200
serious X-Men films don't really acknowledge.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.780
Deadpool himself is the world's biggest critic
of Ryan Reynolds' performance in X-Men Origins:

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.940
Wolverine, which he makes explicitly obvious
during the movie's mid-credits scene by pumping

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.660
a dozen bullets into the other, original Deadpool.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.870
Deadpool 2 does everything it can to try to
weasel itself into the core X-Men movie world.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:51.030
Deadpool wears Professor Xavier's Cerebro
helmet and says that it smells like the original

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.200
X-trilogy's professor Patrick Stewart.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.211
He also zips around the X-Mansion in the Professor's
iconic wheelchair, and most notably, Deadpool

00:00:58.211 --> 00:01:03.360
briefly stands in front of a room filled with
actual, real-life X-Men, including nu-Professor

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.460
X, James McAvoy.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.660
It's the stuff X-Fan dreams are made of.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.360
Essex stuff

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.360
Even though longtime X-Men foe Mr. Sinister,
aka Nathaniel Essex, hasn't actually appeared

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:18.240
on the big screen yet, his presence in the
X-Movie Universe looms large.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.240
So far, he's only appeared in name.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.390
By the end of X-Men: Apocalypse, his name
is seen on a briefcase and is connected to

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.120
the Weapon X facility that created Wolverine.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:32.960
Logan's Transigen corporation, which created
Laura, aka X-23, has been confirmed to be

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.840
related to Essex Corp as well.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.610
In Deadpool 2, Essex is the name of the school
that tortures young mutants, including Russell,

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:41.480
out of using their natural powers.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.920
The bizarro X Mansion seems to be pretty anti-mutant,
so while their ideals don't really align with

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.840
the overall Essex ethos of mutant experimentation,
the name remains a common thread throughout

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.320
all arms of the X-Universe.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.580
Goonies never say die!

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.690
Before he was Thanos, or Cable, or anything
else for that matter, Josh Brolin first appeared

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:05.340
on the big screen as Brand Walsh in The Goonies,
a fact that Deadpool will not let him forget.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.800
Deadpool mockingly calling him One-Eyed Willy
isn't just a dig at his one peeper; One-Eyed

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:13.740
Willy was the pirate that the Goonies were
searching for in their own iconic film.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.200
Want to double down on the Goon-love?

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.880
After Deadpool is torn in two by Juggernaut
and is recovering on the couch, he's wearing

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.060
the exact same shirt that Chunk did in The
Goonies.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.540
It's all done out of love, of course: Ryan
Reynolds and Josh Brolin have a rare kinship.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.700
Both starred in absolutely terrible DC movie
flops, Green Lantern and Jonah Hex, respectively.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:33.480
Callback attack

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.640
Deadpool 2's many commercials revealed a whole
family-sized omelette's worth of Easter eggs,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.060
but a few super clever ones were hidden in
plain sight.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.500
When an exhausted Deadpool attempts to take
out a barrage of bullets with nothing but

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.710
his swords, that's actually a callback to
when he did the exact same thing, with much

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.800
more unbelievable success, in X-Men Origins:
Wolverine.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.280
And yes, Deadpool finding a whole lot of cocaine
and the cure for blindness stashed under some

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.720
floorboards in Al's apartment is an unexpected
callback to the first Deadpool film…

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.660
"There's about 116 kilos of coca cola buried
somewhere in the apartment, right next to

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.600
the cure for blindness .. good luck."

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.240
He wasn't kidding.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.220
Cameo city

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:16.020
Notably absent from Deadpool 2's many crazy
cameos was Marvel patriarch Stan Lee, who

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.380
only appeared as a mural on the side of a
building, and Deadpool 2's teaser trailer.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.720
"Wow, nice suit!"

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.420
"Zip it, Stan Lee!"

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:29.240
One might have also expected Deadpool co-creator
Rob Liefeld to appear again after showing

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.460
up in the first film, but he's nowhere to
be seen.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:32.260
"Buck.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:33.820
Liefeld."

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:39.020
Instead, he's called out in a much more creative
way: as the creator of Domino, who Deadpool

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.840
proclaims to have a very stupid set of powers,
and was probably created by a guy who can't

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:44.340
draw feet.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:49.060
Comic art historians will note that Mr. Liefeld's
grasp of the human anatomy is creative at

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:53.940
its best, hilarious at its worst, and more
often than not, includes large clouds of smoke

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.880
usually focused somewhere below each character's
knees.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.940
"We have a minute and a half left I want to
tighten those feet guys.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:00.440
Okay"

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:00.940
"alright guys?"

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.940
Noted Deadpool writer Gerry Duggan also gets
a shoutout: the parkway on which part of the

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.120
epic convoy battle sequence takes place is
named after him.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.940
And yes, your eyes were not deceiving you:
that indeed was Brad Pitt - playing the ill-fated

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:14.540
Vanisher.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.280
At one point scheduled to play Cable himself,
Pitt's cameo appearance and immediate death

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:22.580
were added in post-production, but are nonetheless
pretty unexpected.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.540
Also unexpected is the surprise appearance
of Alan Tudyk, a toilet-paper obsessed good-ole

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:33.100
boy, appearing alongside Matt Damon, buried
under so many prosthetics that he's unrecognizable.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.660
This, of course, is his second Marvel cameo
over the past year.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:38.340
X-Farce

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.750
The sudden gory departure of almost the entirety
of the newly-formed X-Force is something of

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:45.720
a surprise… but it really shouldn't be.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:50.980
Pretty much the exact same thing happens in
X-Force #116 when a newly formed group of

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:55.960
mutants calling themselves X-Force are immediately,
explosively dispatched, during their very

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:56.900
first appearance.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:02.040
Among them is Zeitgeist, the same tattooed,
acid-puking mutant that's welcomed into Deadpool's

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:02.540
X-Force.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:07.180
That tattoo of 116 on his arm wasn't just
an interstate, it's the issue where he died.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.360
And we can't mention highly-specific numerical
references without Deadpool's prisoner number:

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:13.060
24601.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:15.419
"Now prisoner 24601…"

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.240
Most significantly, it's the number of Jean
Valjean in Les Miserables, a role played by

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:21.960
Hugh Jackman on the big screen.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:23.420
Bad taste

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.360
Deadpool co-star TJ Miller has been embroiled
in multiple controversies since December of

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.840
2016, including, but not limited to, calling
in a fake a bomb threat on a train, assaulting

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:39.400
an Uber driver during a political argument,
and allegedly committing violence and sexual

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:41.760
assault against a woman in 2001.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:45.991
When the latter accusations surfaced after
Miller's prominent role in Deadpool 2 was

00:05:45.991 --> 00:05:50.830
already in production, the film's producers
made the difficult and controversial decision

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:54.680
to keep the Emoji Movie star in the picture,
but axe him from Deadpool 3.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.020
Still, the film couldn't resist taking a jab
at the situation.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:02.700
During the news report about Firefists' explosive
meltdown, the crawl at the bottom of the screen

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:06.360
mentions that Christopher Plummer had turned
down a role in Deadpool 2.

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:11.090
Famously, Plummer reshot all of Kevin Spacey's
scenes in All the Money in the World after

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:13.860
Kevin Spacey was accused of sexual misconduct.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.860
It's not like anything in Deadpool 2 was in
good taste anyhow.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:19.400
"Noooo!"

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.380
Good taste

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:25.790
It's hard to tell exactly where Deadpool ends
and Ryan Reynolds begins, and it's a line

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:30.050
that's blurred constantly: Deadpool signs
a Wolverine cereal box with Reynolds' name,

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:33.360
and later shoots Reynolds through the head
as he considers ever even thinking about appearing

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:34.160
in Green Lantern.

00:06:34.449 --> 00:06:38.320
But despite all of the movie's leaps from
different realities and tasteless jokes, one

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.940
throwaway line has more heart than the whole
film combined.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:44.840
When Wade Wilson considers the name Connor
for his kid, it's not meaningless.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:48.650
Prior to the original Deadpool coming out,
Ryan Reynolds enjoyed a special screening

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:51.300
of the film with a Make-a-Wish kid named Connor
McGrath.

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:55.980
While Connor passed in 2016, Reynolds made
sure that his love of Deadpool and his legacy

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:00.380
lived on in Deadpool 2 in the most heartwarming
and heartbreaking way possible.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:02.060
Costume connection

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.320
While Deadpool is mostly known for his red-and-black
duds, he's fought crime in a wide variety

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:07.960
of colors.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:13.270
While seeking membership in the X-Men during
Deadpool #16, he actually makes his own X-Men

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:15.660
uniform, with a giant, obvious X on the front.

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:20.560
Whoever designed the X-Men's trainee uniforms
in Deadpool 2 had pretty much the same idea.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.790
Deadpool, during his darker X-Force days,
has also been seen sporting a black and grey

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:26.620
version of his familiar costume.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:31.180
His charred costume near the end of the movie
is a dead ringer for his X-Force comics counterpart.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.140
It's no coincidence: this is when the real
X-Force finally comes together.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:36.400
Sorry, Shatterstar.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:37.100
You suck.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:38.860
O Canada

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:43.400
Because you just can't have Ryan Reynolds
in the room without making a few jokes about

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:47.710
his home and native land up north, Deadpool
2 has a few quips about America's friendly

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:48.560
hat, Canada.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:53.260
Much more subtle, however, is a reference,
in name only, to Canada's number one crime-fighting

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:55.320
team, the never-mentioned Alpha Flight.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:59.880
In this case, Alpha Flight is little more
than the name of an airline or travel agency

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.940
posted as a taxi ad, but the sentiment is
there.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:05.420
And it's covered in maple syrup and hockey.

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:10.000
Thanks for watching!

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:12.600
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.080
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

